                                <div class="col-12 col-lg-8"><p><strong>Position:</strong> California 38th Senate District</p><div>Senator Catherine Blakespear represents California’s 38th Sen
f34
ate District, serving coastal communities across northern San Diego and southern Orange Counties. She is Chair of the Senate Environmental Quality Committee, the first woman ever to hold the position. Under her leadership the committee evaluated more than 120 bills in 2025 alone affecting California’s air, water, and the products Californians use every day, and she helped craft the landmark renewal of California’s cap-and-invest program, which holds the state’s largest polluters accountable and&nbsp; directs the proceeds toward clean transportation, clean air, and clean water.<div><br></div><div>Senator Blakespear has authored 24 bills signed into law, including legislation banning plastic bags at grocery store checkouts and protecting the property tax exclusion for rooftop solar and battery storage.</div><div><br></div><div>This session she authored SB 954, restoring environmental and public health guardrails weakened by last year’s overly broad CEQA exemption for industrial manufacturing. She carried it through both houses with a coalition including California Environmental Voters, the Natural Resources Defense Council, and Earthjustice. She also helped drive the Legislature’s response to the Tijuana River Valley pollution crisis, chairing a joint Senate and Assembly hearing and writing SB 1046 to protect lifeguards, park rangers, and other workers exposed to toxic cross-border pollution. Both bills await the Governor’s signature.</div><div><br></div><div>Sierra Club California has endorsed her re-election, and the Sierra Club Angeles Chapter honored her with its 2026 Political Leadership Award.</div><div><br></div><div>Before the Senate, she served six years as Mayor of Encinitas, where she adopted an ambitious Climate Action Plan and moved the city to 100 percent renewable power.</div></div></div></div></div></div>
